  "prompt_text": "The figure compares three conceptual ways silicic magma reservoirs can be organized in the crust above repeatedly injected mafic magmas. In all cases, magma rises from below the base of the crust into a larger gray reservoir containing a crystal-rich mush and melt-rich regions, with eruptions tapping the upper part.\n\nThe main contrast is reservoir architecture. One model shows several separate melt-dominant pockets that are compositionally distinct, sitting above a shared deeper mush and mafic input zone. Another shows a single body that is compositionally stratified, with less evolved magma lower in the chamber and more evolved magma concentrated at the top, while crystal transfer between mush and melt-rich zones appears limited. The third shows a single unzoned body, where compositional differences are produced mainly by mixing or rejuvenation rather than stable layering.\n\nAcross all three sketches, mafic recharge from below seems to promote mixing, mush remobilization, and occasional rejuvenation of cumulates. Some labels also indicate pre-eruptive or syn-eruptive mingling between mafic and rhyolitic magmas. Overall, the figure emphasizes that similar volcanic systems may store magma as either multiple discrete lenses, one layered chamber, or one more homogenized reservoir shaped by recharge and mixing.",
